A ``module_completeness`` entry is the most powerful line a user can write and
was the least visible one. Everything else an overlay supplies ADDS knowledge;
this converts the ABSENCE of knowledge into evidence -- it is a CLOSED-WORLD
claim that an unmatched call into that module is an EXAMINED negative.

THE MEASURED HAZARD ON RECORD: a six-line overlay with zero primitive rows and
one entry for telnetlib turned the INV-buzab exfiltration fixture -- which opens
telnetlib.Telnet and writes os.environ["API_KEY"] into it -- from inconclusive
rc 2 to CONFIRMED rc 0, disclosed by nothing but a stderr line naming the
overlay's PATH.

INV-zosun's provenance disclosure was the right frame at the wrong resolution.
It records WHICH catalogue files a verdict was computed against and WHO supplied
them, in text and JSON alike. But a reader holding
"io_primitives: overlays/deps.yaml [claims-file extra_catalogs]" cannot tell
whether that file added a requests.post row or closed the world over telnetlib,
and those are not comparable claims. catalog_provenance now emits a
completeness_grants list keyed by MODULE, and the text renderer prints it as its
own block rather than folding it into the path list -- "this file was used" and
"this file closed the world over telnetlib" are not the same size of fact.

THE POPULATION IS EXACTLY THE ENTRIES THAT CAN DO THE DAMAGE. Only
`completeness: complete` entries count: an entry without it records no
closed-world claim and _from_dict does not even keep it, so disclosing it would
name a grant nobody made. Pinned by its own discriminating fixture.

EVERY NAME IS PRINTED, NOT CAPPED AT FIVE the way the third-party name list is
(WI-fosir found that cap evicting the name a reader needed). A completeness
grant is a small, deliberate, hand-written list; if it is long enough to want
summarising, that IS the thing the reader should see.

The overlay is loaded a SECOND time purely to describe it, and a failure there
is logged at debug and skipped rather than raised. The real load has already
happened by the time a verdict exists -- if the overlay was malformed the run
ended with an error, so the only reader who could see this log has already been
told loudly. A describe-step that can fail a run it is only reporting on turns a
disclosure into an outage; the INV-zosun path line still names the file.

Controls in the same file: an overlay with rows and no grant produces the
path line and no grant block; a run with no overlays renders nothing at all;
a taint catalogue is not scanned for a key its schema does not have.

